  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1131 PM CDT SUN OCT 4 2026\n\nRain will continue to gradually clear the region overnight, as\nthe cold front keeps its southern track. This will result in\nmostly dry conditions across the state beginning today, sans a\nfew remaining showers along I-85. These dry conditions are \nexpected for most of the upcoming week, with the cold front \nquickly retrograding back north on Tuesday. This will allow for \ntemperatures to slightly rebound, with highs settling into the low\nto mid-80s. Thankfully, overnight lows will still fall to a \nseasonable feel, with cooler air filtering into the region via the\nAppalachian Mountains into Friday.\n\nThe forecast for much of the workweek will remain in this steady\nstate, before conditions break down into the weekend. Long range\nguidance is already in pretty good agreement regarding some kind\nof shift in the synoptic pattern by Friday. Some guidance is far\nmore aggressive than others, but the general consensus is a weak\nlow pressure forming over central Texas, interacting with\ndisturbances in the Gulf. This would usher in additional Gulf\nmoisture, increasing rain chances as these disturbances move\ninland. While the signal is here regarding some kind of pattern\nbreakdown, confidence on the exact outcome is low given the wide\nspread in current outcomes.\n\n/44/",
